{"name":"Surprise Amber","hex":"#efb57a","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"surprise","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Something unexpected.","A mess of feces, left by a pet or small child in an unexpected place or at an unexpected time."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cause (someone) to feel unusually alarmed or delighted by something unexpected.","To do something to (a person) that they are not expecting, as a surprise."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/surprise"},{"term":"amber","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Ambergris, the waxy product of the sperm whale. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}\n\nFormerly thought to be the product of a plant.","Formerly thought to be the product of a plant."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a brownish yellow colour, like that of most amber."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/amber"}],"entries":[{"title":"Surprise","blocks":[{"text":"Surprise is a rapid, fleeting mental and physiological state. It is related to the startle response experienced by animals and humans as the result of an unexpected event."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Charles_Bell%2C_Essays_on_the_anatomy_of_Wellcome_L0031951.jpg/330px-Charles_Bell%2C_Essays_on_the_anatomy_of_Wellcome_L0031951.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Surprise","wikidata":"Q333173"},{"title":"Amber","blocks":[{"text":"Amber is fossilized tree resin. It has been appreciated for its color and natural beauty since Neolithic times, and worked as a gemstone since classical antiquity. Amber is used in jewelry and as a healing agent in folk medicine."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Amber2.jpg/330px-Amber2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amber","wikidata":"Q25381"},{"title":"Amber (color)","blocks":[{"text":"The color amber is a pure chroma color, located on the color wheel midway between the colors of yellow and orange. The color name is derived from the material also known as amber, which is commonly found in a range of yellow-orange-brown-red colors; likewise, as a color, amber can refer to a range of yellow-orange colors. In English, the first recorded use of the term as a color name, rather than a reference to the specific substance, was in 1500."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Ant_in_amber_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Ant_in_amber_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amber_(color)","wikidata":"Q679355"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/STS-51L_riadiace_stredisko.jpg/500px-STS-51L_riadiace_stredisko.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/STS-51L_riadiace_stredisko.jpg/1280px-STS-51L_riadiace_stredisko.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/STS-51L_riadiace_stredisko.jpg/500px-STS-51L_riadiace_stredisko.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" A surprised NASA Engineer Jay Greene. Photo taken after the destruction of the Challenger Space shuttle in 1986","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASTS-51L_riadiace_stredisko.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Carol_Burnett_1958.JPG/500px-Carol_Burnett_1958.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Carol_Burnett_1958.JPG/500px-Carol_Burnett_1958.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Comedian Carol Burn